# Line endings, which on this project are not cosmetic.
#
# Every live test is a **bash script** (`pkg/livetest/*run.sh`, `coretest.sh`, `build.sh`), and a
# shell script with CRLF endings does not fail loudly: bash reads the `\r` as part of the last word
# on each line, so `do` becomes `do\r`, `then` becomes `then\r`, and `if [ -f "$X" ]; then` stops
# matching - a suite that exits with a syntax error whose message points at a line that looks
# perfect. Git's `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows converts on checkout, so without this file a fresh
# clone on a Windows machine gets a test suite that cannot run.
#
# `text=auto` normalizes on the way in; `eol=lf` is what a checkout gets back, for every text file.
# Directory-level rules first, then the exceptions - a `.exe` or a `.zip` is not text and must never
# be "normalized" (it is rewritten, and the file is then corrupt).
* text=auto eol=lf

*.sh   text eol=lf
*.java text eol=lf
*.js   text eol=lf
*.cs   text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf

# Anything already compressed or compiled: byte-for-byte or not at all.
*.zip  binary
*.jar  binary
*.exe  binary
*.dll  binary
*.png  binary
*.ico  binary
*.cer  binary
*.bbmodel binary
